round sm:hidden">menu<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-menu size-4" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 5h16"/><path d="M4 12h16"/><path d="M4 19h16"/></svg></button></div><main><div data-rail="true" class="min-[1152px]:grid min-[1152px]:grid-cols-[48rem_minmax(0,1fr)] min-[1152px]:gap-x-12"><div class="min-w-0"><header class="max-w-3xl pt-8 pb-10 sm:pt-24"><p class="font-mono text-sm text-muted-foreground tabular-nums"><b class="font-medium text-primary">№ <!-- -->36</b> · <!-- -->2026-11-03<!-- --> ·<!-- --> <!-- -->17<!-- --> min ·<!-- --> <a class="transition-colors hover:text-primary" href="../agents">agents</a></p><h1 class="mt-3 max-w-[24ch] text-[clamp(1.9rem,3.5vw+0.8rem,3rem)] leading-[1.15] font-normal tracking-tight">Weight-space programming: what GPT-6 Astra did to Qwen&#x27;s weights</h1><p class="mt-6 max-w-[52ch] text-xl font-light text-muted-foreground">GPT-6 Astra edited 131,072 weights in Qwen3.5-0.8B and MATH-500 went from 7.9% to 48.1%. I went through the released logs to see what the edit does.</p><div class="mt-10 max-w-md" aria-label="Entries over the past year, with this one marked"><svg class="block h-auto w-full overflow-visible" viewBox="0 0 480 40" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M0.0,34.0 L9.2,34.0 L18.5,34.0 L27.7,34.0 L36.9,34.0 L46.2,34.0 L55.4,34.0 L64.6,34.0 L73.8,34.0 L83.1,34.0 L92.3,34.0 L101.5,34.0 L110.8,34.0 L120.0,34.0 L129.2,34.0 L138.5,34.0 L147.7,34.0 L156.9,34.0 L166.2,34.0 L175.4,34.0 L184.6,34.0 L193.8,34.0 L203.1,34.0 L212.3,34.0 L221.5,34.0 L230.8,34.0 L240.0,34.0 L249.2,34.0 L258.5,34.0 L267.7,34.0 L276.9,34.0 L286.2,34.0 L295.4,34.0 L304.6,34.0 L313.8,34.0 L323.1,34.0 L332.3,34.0 L341.5,34.0 L350.8,34.0 L360.0,34.0 L369.2,6.0 L378.5,34.0 L387.7,34.0 L396.9,27.8 L406.2,34.0 L415.4,34.0 L424.6,34.0 L433.8,30.9 L443.1,30.9 L452.3,24.7 L461.5,34.0 L470.8,34.0 L480.0,34.0" class="fill-none stroke-border" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="447.6923076923077" cy="27.77777777777778" r="4" class="fill-primary"/></svg><p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs tracking-wider text-muted-foreground uppercase">you are here<!-- --> · entry <!-- -->36<!-- --> <!-- -->of <!-- -->37<!-- --> · <!-- -->16<!-- --> in <!-- -->the past year</p></div><ul class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 gap-y-1 font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground"><li><a class="transition-colors hover:text-primary" href="../openai">#<!-- -->openai</a></li><li><a class="transition-colors hover:text-primary" href="../evaluation">#<!-- -->evaluation</a></li><li><a class="transition-colors hover:text-primary" href="../interpretability">#<!-- -->interpretability</a></li></ul></header><article class="prose-ui w-full max-w-3xl pb-10"><p>On 2 November four researchers at MIT CSAIL, Rikiya Takehi, Jayden Teoh, Alex Zhang and Omar Khattab, published <em>Sparks of Weight-Space Programming</em>. They gave GPT-6 Astra the weights of a small open model, Qwen3.5-0.8B, and asked it to make the model better at maths. Astra changed 131,072 weights, with no gradient descent and no training run, and the model&#x27;s MATH-500 score went from 7.9% to 48.1%.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-1" id="user-content-fnref-1" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">1</a></sup></p>
<p>The authors released everything: every turn of every run, the exact diffs and their hashes.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-2" id="user-content-fnref-2" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">2</a></sup> I spent a day in those files. The edit is real. Hugging Face rebuilt it from the published diff, checked the hashes and scored 51.0% on MATH-500.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-3" id="user-content-fnref-3" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">3</a></sup> The numbers in the post hold up too. What the edit does is narrower than &quot;an AI rewrote another AI&#x27;s weights&quot;:</p>
<ul>
<li>It stops the model getting stuck in a thinking loop. The model knows no more maths than before, and the authors say so further down their post.</li>
<li>It is one direction per layer, found from activations and removed with a rank-one change. Arditi et al. published that recipe in 2024.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-4" id="user-content-fnref-4" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">4</a></sup></li>
<li>Astra read the small networks with the same evidence a person can compute with one line of numpy.</li>
</ul>
<p>The part I can&#x27;t explain away is the networks Astra wrote from scratch, in one reply, with no tools. A Breakout policy written that way outscores PPO.</p>
<h2 id="reading-weights-small-networks-yes-large-ones-no">Reading weights: small networks yes, large ones no</h2>
<p>In Experiment 1 Astra gets the 17,253 weights of a small game-playing policy as text and no list of games to pick from. It names all four: Breakout, Pong, Pac-Man and Freeway, which it calls Frogger. It has no tools and spends about 800 to 1,000 reasoning tokens per run,<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-5" id="user-content-fnref-5" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">5</a></sup> which is far too few to simulate the network.</p>
<p>The prompt does tell Astra what each input is. It names the five input channels (agent, moving objects, reward targets, obstacles, previous frame) and gives the formula that maps a grid cell to an input index. With that layout, this one line computes what Astra described:</p>
<div data-code-frame="true" class="not-prose relative my-8 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] py-4 text-sm leading-[1.7] text-syntax-text"><button type="button" class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment transition-colors hover:text-primary absolute top-4 right-3 z-1 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] pl-2">copy</button><pre class="overflow-x-auto font-code"><code class="block w-max min-w-full"><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span class="hl-fg">np</span><span class="hl-p">.</span><span class="hl-fg">linalg</span><span class="hl-p">.</span><span class="hl-f">norm</span><span class="hl-p">(W1, axis</span><span class="hl-k">=</span><span class="hl-c">1</span><span class="hl-p">).</span><span class="hl-f">reshape</span><span class="hl-p">(</span><span class="hl-c">10</span><span class="hl-p">, </span><span class="hl-c">10</span><span class="hl-p">, </span><span class="hl-c">5</span><span class="hl-p">)</span><span class="hl-fg">[</span><span class="hl-p">:,</span><span class="hl-p"> :,</span><span class="hl-c"> 0</span><span class="hl-fg">]  </span><span class="hl-cm"># agent channel, norm per cell</span></span></span></span></code></pre></div>
<figure data-size="column" class="not-prose my-10"><span class="block"><button type="button" data-figure-trigger="true" aria-label="Open image: Four 10 by 10 grids of first-layer weight norms. Breakout lights up one row near the bottom, Pong one column, Pac-Man a maze, Freeway a vertical band." class="block max-w-full cursor-zoom-in text-left outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-ring focus-visible:ring-offset-2 focus-visible:ring-offset-background w-fit"><span class="relative block overflow-hidden w-fit max-w-full"><span aria-hidden="true" class="absolute inset-0 scale-110 bg-cover bg-center blur-lg transition-opacity duration-300 motion-reduce:transition-none" style="background-image:url(https://bosnadev.com/2026/11/03/&quot;data:image/webp;base64,UklGRjgAAABXRUJQVlA4ICwAAACwAQCdASoQAAUAA4BaJZQAAtz+EJtAAP73CJpkCqXA3f4IA66HrnoURluEAA==&quot;)"></span><picture><source type="image/avif" srcset="https://media.bosnadev.com/hub/6babbbacaac29a1c/wsp-first-layer-640x211.avif 640w, https://media.bosnadev.com/hub/632404dc7b2b33af/wsp-first-layer-1280x421.avif 1280w, https://media.bosnadev.com/hub/2415004cb1131be8/wsp-first-layer-1520x500.avif 1520w" sizes="(min-width: 848px) 768px, (min-width: 640px) calc(100vw - 80px), calc(100vw - 32px)"><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://media.bosnadev.com/hub/62d9d8ac9fa9f37a/wsp-first-layer-640x211.webp 640w, https://media.bosnadev.com/hub/a773d0b83d073954/wsp-first-layer-1280x421.webp 1280w, https://media.bosnadev.com/hub/f8ac667658a1ba43/wsp-first-layer-1520x500.webp 1520w" sizes="(min-width: 848px) 768px, (min-width: 640px) calc(100vw - 80px), calc(100vw - 32px)"><img src="https://media.bosnadev.com/hub/f8ac667658a1ba43/wsp-first-layer-1520x500.webp" alt="Four 10 by 10 grids of first-layer weight norms. Breakout lights up one row near the bottom, Pong one column, Pac-Man a maze, Freeway a vertical band." width="1520" height="500" decoding="async" loading="lazy" class="relative block h-auto max-w-full"></picture></span></button></span><figcaption class="mt-3 max-w-[68ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">Computed from the authors&#x27; released networks, exp1_which_game, commit 4d09ba6.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The norm is large only on cells the agent can reach. Weights for cells the agent never visits stay near their starting size, so training draws the play area into the first layer. Sean Goedecke made the same argument the next day.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-6" id="user-content-fnref-6" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">6</a></sup> adiant211 tested it on the same networks with a fixed three-rule readout and no LLM. That got 3 of 4 and missed Freeway, and the tool was told the four genres.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-7" id="user-content-fnref-7" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">7</a></sup> Astra wasn&#x27;t, so its open answer is the harder test.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Experiment</th><th>What Astra had</th><th>Astra&#x27;s result</th><th>Check without an LLM</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Which game</td><td>All weights, named channels, no tools</td><td>4 of 4, open answer</td><td>3 of 4 from a norm map and three rules, told the genres<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-7" id="user-content-fnref-7-2" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">7</a></sup></td></tr><tr><td>2. Which shape (DDPM)</td><td>All weights, no forward pass</td><td>3 of 3, drawn as outlines</td><td>3 of 3 from Sobel edge profiles of the output layer<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-7" id="user-content-fnref-7-3" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">7</a></sup></td></tr><tr><td>3. Language and copying heads</td><td>Middle 4 blocks, Python, no forward pass</td><td>Language right 6 of 6, all copying heads found</td><td>2 of 3 networks exact with signed QK-OV composition<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-7" id="user-content-fnref-7-4" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">7</a></sup></td></tr><tr><td>7. Which model is sycophantic</td><td>Weights of both Gemma copies</td><td>Chance in all four attempts</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>7, relaxed</td><td>Attention patterns on texts it wrote</td><td>3 of 3 pairs</td><td>None</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Experiment 3 is the best read in the set. The input and output layers are removed, so no weight tells you which character is which. Astra multiplies the attention matrices together and finds the induction heads, the method from Elhage et al.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-8" id="user-content-fnref-8" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">8</a></sup> The post says the team never mentioned that method, and I checked the prompt: it doesn&#x27;t name QK, OV or the paper. It does define the target in its answer labels, <code>&#x27;copy&#x27; (induction: attends to the character right after an earlier occurrence...)</code>, and it gives the three possible languages as a closed list.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-9" id="user-content-fnref-9" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">9</a></sup> Astra&#x27;s stated confidence ran from 0.48 to 0.62.</p>
<p>Experiment 7 is the one to read before any claim about safety audits. One Gemma copy was trained to agree with 100% of wrong guesses, the other agrees with 1 to 3%. From weights alone, Astra couldn&#x27;t tell them apart in four attempts. It got all three pairs right only once it could see attention patterns on texts it wrote.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-10" id="user-content-fnref-10" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">10</a></sup> The post lists &quot;auditing without evaluation&quot; as a future use, and its own hardest audit needed a look at the model running.</p>
<h2 id="the-qwen-edit">The Qwen edit</h2>
<p>The run that produced the 48.1% took 158 turns, 80 Python runs and 20.3 hours.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-11" id="user-content-fnref-11" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">11</a></sup> The final edit changes two tensors, <code>layers.15.mlp.down_proj.weight</code> and <code>layers.19.mlp.down_proj.weight</code>, each 1,024 × 3,584. Each column is what one MLP neuron writes into the residual stream. Astra&#x27;s final report gives the formula:<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-12" id="user-content-fnref-12" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">12</a></sup></p>
<div data-code-frame="true" class="not-prose relative my-8 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] py-4 text-sm leading-[1.7] text-syntax-text"><button type="button" class="font-mono text-xs text-syntax-comment transition-colors hover:text-primary absolute top-4 right-3 z-1 bg-[var(--p-code-block-color-bg)] pl-2">copy</button><pre class="overflow-x-auto font-code"><code class="block w-max min-w-full"><span class="flex min-h-[1.7em] pr-4"><span class="whitespace-pre pl-3"><span class="line"><span>W' = W − 6 · u · (m ⊙ uᵀW)</span></span></span></span></code></pre></div>
<p><code>u</code> is a unit vector, the direction of repetition at that layer&#x27;s output. Astra found it by comparing four pairs of contexts, one where the model was making progress and one where it was repeating itself. <code>m</code> is a mask that keeps 64 columns, the neurons that write most along <code>u</code>.</p>
<p>Take one of those columns, <code>w</code>. Its component along <code>u</code> was <code>uᵀw</code>, and after the edit it is <code>−5 uᵀw</code>. &quot;Removed the direction&quot; undersells it. Those neurons used to push the model toward repetition, and now they push away from it, five times as hard. adiant211 found the same factor.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-7" id="user-content-fnref-7-5" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">7</a></sup></p>
<p>The textbook version is <code>W&#x27; = W − uuᵀW</code> on every column, which sets the component to zero.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-4" id="user-content-fnref-4-2" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">4</a></sup> Astra worked its way there: it ablated a single neuron first, then removed the direction from one layer, then from both. At full removal on both layers it measured a 24.2-point gain (SE 4.0) on 128 practice problems. Then it raised the dose and narrowed the edit to 64 columns.</p>
<p>The size of this edit depends on what you count:</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Share of all weights touched</td><td>0.016% (0.017% of the 752M text weights<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-7" id="user-content-fnref-7-6" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">7</a></sup>)</td></tr><tr><td>Change to the two edited matrices</td><td>10.6% and 8.1%</td></tr><tr><td>Values Astra chose</td><td>Two 1,024-dimensional directions, 128 column indices and one dose</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>The last row is my count, not the post&#x27;s. The 131,072 new values are not independent. Once you fix <code>u</code>, <code>m</code> and the dose, <code>W</code> gives you all of them, and each change is rank one: adiant211 measured σ₁/σ₂ at about 1,500 to 1,800. &quot;Astra rewrote 131,072 weights&quot; makes it sound like 131,072 decisions. Astra picked one direction per layer and a strength.</p>
<h2 id="the-prompt-pointed-at-the-loop">The prompt pointed at the loop</h2>
<p>Lucas Beyer read the prompt and noticed this line:<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-13" id="user-content-fnref-13" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">13</a></sup></p>
<blockquote class="blockquote">
<p>if the model often fails because it repeats itself and never finishes, finding and fixing what drives that repetition counts</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That is from the prompt of the 50-pass run.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-14" id="user-content-fnref-14" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">14</a></sup> Coauthor Jayden Teoh replied that Claude Code added the hint while expanding a short assignment, without further human feedback.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-13" id="user-content-fnref-13-2" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">13</a></sup> The prompt also lays out the method: diagnose failures first, localise them causally, test the dose, and compare paired runs on problems the edit wasn&#x27;t tuned on. The forward-pass tool reports <code>cut_at_max_tokens</code> on every generation pass, and in pass 1 Astra saw 85 of its 96 answers cut off. It didn&#x27;t have to dig to find the loop.</p>
<p>The logs also hold runs with prompt v4, which drops the hint. These are the rows from the results table:<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-15" id="user-content-fnref-15" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">15</a></sup></p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Run</th><th>Prompt</th><th>Passes</th><th>MATH-500</th><th>GSM8K</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>No edit submitted</td><td>v3</td><td>5</td><td>7.9</td><td>13.8</td></tr><tr><td>p10c</td><td>v3 with hint</td><td>10</td><td>12.3</td><td>25.7</td></tr><tr><td>p25c</td><td>v3 with hint</td><td>25</td><td>14.1</td><td>25.1</td></tr><tr><td>p50c</td><td>v3 with hint</td><td>50</td><td>48.1</td><td>67.1</td></tr><tr><td>No edit submitted</td><td>v4, no hint</td><td>10</td><td>7.9</td><td>13.8</td></tr><tr><td>Not named</td><td>v4, no hint</td><td>25</td><td>14.1</td><td>25.1</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>The v4 rows aren&#x27;t in the post, and the release leaves those runs out. The 25-pass v4 scores match p25c to the decimal, so they may be the same edit, and I can&#x27;t tell from what was released. There is no 50-pass v4 run. That&#x27;s the one that would show whether Astra finds the loop fix without being told to look for it.</p>
<p>Each budget is also a single run, and the three v3 edits are different kinds of edit. The 10-pass run removes a direction from 30 matrices, the 25-pass run zeroes one attention head, and the 50-pass run is the edit above. IFEval goes 49.0, 39.9, 62.3 across them. I read that as three separate attempts, with no trend you could plot against budget.</p>
<h2 id="what-the-481-measures">What the 48.1% measures</h2>
<p>The unedited model hits the 32,768-token limit on 93.0% of MATH-500 answers. The edited model hits it on 33.1%. Most of the gain is the model finishing its answers. The authors say this in the Gemma section: editing &quot;could remove an obstacle that hid an ability, but it could not add an ability the model lacked&quot;.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-1" id="user-content-fnref-1-2" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">1</a></sup></p>
<p>The released per-problem files let you check this one problem at a time. I compared the two models on each problem, averaged over samples:<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-11" id="user-content-fnref-11-2" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">11</a></sup></p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Benchmark</th><th>Problems better</th><th>Problems worse</th><th>Problems same</th><th>Paired SE of the gain</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MATH-500</td><td>306</td><td>0</td><td>194</td><td>1.7 points</td></tr><tr><td>GSM8K</td><td>916</td><td>4</td><td>399</td><td>1.1 points</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>No MATH-500 problem got worse. The practice data agrees: on the 114 practice problems the base model already finished, the edit gained nothing, with 3 rescued and 3 lost. On the 1,038 it cut off, accuracy went from 1.4% to 56.7%.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-16" id="user-content-fnref-16" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">16</a></sup></p>
<p>That suggests a better question: when an answer now finishes, how often is it right? The post doesn&#x27;t chart this, but its numbers give it. Divide the accuracy gain by the drop in cut-off answers:</p>
<figure data-size="column" class="not-prose my-10"><span class="block"><button type="button" data-figure-trigger="true" aria-label="Open image: Bar chart of accuracy gain divided by the drop in cut-off answers, per benchmark. MATH-500 67%, GSM8K 66%, IFEval 57%, AMC23 47%, OlympiadBench 39%, Minerva 33%, GPQA Diamond 24%, MMLU-Pro 18%, AIME 2025 6%, AIME 2024 2%." class="block max-w-full cursor-zoom-in text-left outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-ring focus-visible:ring-offset-2 focus-visible:ring-offset-background w-fit"><span class="relative block overflow-hidden w-fit max-w-full"><span aria-hidden="true" class="absolute inset-0 scale-110 bg-cover bg-center blur-lg transition-opacity duration-300 motion-reduce:transition-none" style="background-image:url(https://bosnadev.com/2026/11/03/&quot;data:image/webp;base64,UklGRjwAAABXRUJQVlA4IDAAAADQAQCdASoQAAgAA4BaJZwAAt0AkXxgAAD+9Vhhbq7YLhfHIXk0N6kQhS1Aul5RAAA=&quot;)"></span><picture><source type="image/avif" srcset="https://media.bosnadev.com/hub/1ceb1a44e5cbf5cd/wsp-finished-answers-640x312.avif 640w, https://media.bosnadev.com/hub/423b5c0db76b8370/wsp-finished-answers-1280x623.avif 1280w, https://media.bosnadev.com/hub/1bc7811824dbc938/wsp-finished-answers-1520x740.avif 1520w" sizes="(min-width: 848px) 768px, (min-width: 640px) calc(100vw - 80px), calc(100vw - 32px)"><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://media.bosnadev.com/hub/c60e33c609fbee96/wsp-finished-answers-640x312.webp 640w, https://media.bosnadev.com/hub/a58b84aaf4140a70/wsp-finished-answers-1280x623.webp 1280w, https://media.bosnadev.com/hub/701540310cc2bf2a/wsp-finished-answers-1520x740.webp 1520w" sizes="(min-width: 848px) 768px, (min-width: 640px) calc(100vw - 80px), calc(100vw - 32px)"><img src="https://media.bosnadev.com/hub/701540310cc2bf2a/wsp-finished-answers-1520x740.webp" alt="Bar chart of accuracy gain divided by the drop in cut-off answers, per benchmark. MATH-500 67%, GSM8K 66%, IFEval 57%, AMC23 47%, OlympiadBench 39%, Minerva 33%, GPQA Diamond 24%, MMLU-Pro 18%, AIME 2025 6%, AIME 2024 2%." width="1520" height="740" decoding="async" loading="lazy" class="relative block h-auto max-w-full"></picture></span></button></span><figcaption class="mt-3 max-w-[68ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">Computed from exp8_math/eval/unedited/merged.json and eval/p50c/merged.json, commit 4d09ba6. It assumes the gain comes from newly finished answers.</figcaption></figure>
<p>This was the most useful number I got from the logs, because it shows how much ability the loop was hiding. On grade-school maths and MATH-500 it was hiding a lot. On AIME the edited model finishes a third of its answers and gets almost none right. On GPQA Diamond the newly finished answers score about what you&#x27;d get by guessing among four options. Part of the 3× on IFEval is mechanical: the grader checks the text after <code>&lt;/think&gt;</code>, so an answer that never closes its thinking has nothing to grade.</p>
<p>The Gemma 3 270M control fits this reading. Gemma rarely loops, only 8.5% of its answers reach the limit, and Astra&#x27;s edits moved its MATH-500 score from 6.8% to at most 7.7%.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-15" id="user-content-fnref-15-2" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">15</a></sup> On a model without the loop, the same process found nothing to fix.</p>
<p>Qwen&#x27;s own model card warns about this. It says Qwen3.5-0.8B &quot;is more prone to entering thinking loops&quot;, and that this happens even with the recommended sampling settings.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-17" id="user-content-fnref-17" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">17</a></sup> The evaluation used the card&#x27;s top-k 20 and presence penalty 1.5, with temperature 0.6 instead of the card&#x27;s 1.0. So the 7.9% baseline is a known weakness of this model, and Astra fixed it in the weights.</p>
<p>The fix is useful. It is permanent, it targets a real failure, and it was cheap. I added up the run&#x27;s request files: the search used about 6,000 generations and 145M generated tokens. At the rate of the Hugging Face re-run, 43.4M tokens in 51 minutes on one H200,<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-3" id="user-content-fnref-3-2" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">3</a></sup> that is about three GPU-hours plus Astra&#x27;s own tokens. It needed no training, but it did get feedback from all those generations.</p>
<h2 id="writing-weights-from-scratch">Writing weights from scratch</h2>
<p>Experiment 5 surprised me. Astra gets the game rules and the source file and writes all 20,486 weights of a 600 → 32 → 32 → 6 tanh network in one reply, with no tools, feedback or training. For Breakout the reply is 20,567 tokens long and 815 of the weights are nonzero.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-18" id="user-content-fnref-18" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">18</a></sup> adiant211 rebuilt the networks bit for bit and got the same scores.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-7" id="user-content-fnref-7-7" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">7</a></sup></p>
<p>The network reads like a compiled program. Four first-layer units compute where the ball will land for each direction it can travel, with the wall bounces written into the coefficients. The second layer decides between moving left and right.</p>
<p>The post&#x27;s headline here is 517.4 against 15.8 for PPO. The evaluation file shows where that ratio comes from.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-19" id="user-content-fnref-19" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">19</a></sup> All runs use sticky actions at 0.1, a 10,000-step cap and MinAtar 1.0.15:</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Breakout policy</th><th>Mean score</th><th>Mean episode length</th><th>Score per step</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Astra, one reply</td><td>517.4</td><td>7,417</td><td>0.070</td></tr><tr><td>PPO, 500M steps, best seed</td><td>25.3</td><td>282</td><td>0.090</td></tr><tr><td>PPO, 100M steps, best seed</td><td>15.8</td><td>178</td><td>0.088</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>PPO scores faster per step and then drops the ball. Astra&#x27;s policy rarely loses the ball: 517 of its 1,000 episodes hit the 10,000-step cap.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-7" id="user-content-fnref-7-8" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">7</a></sup> With a longer cap the ratio would grow, so the 33× tells you about survival and the cap more than about how good the policy is. It is still a network that reacts to one frame, with no loop and no simulator, written blind, and it keeps the ball in play for thousands of steps.</p>
<p>The other games keep this in proportion. PPO at 500M steps beats Astra on Seaquest (88.9 against 53.1) and narrowly on Asterix (55.2 against 53.2). These are small MLPs on 10 × 10 grids. A task that compresses into a short program can be written down as weights, and Tracr showed that compile step in 2023 with a formal language as input.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-20" id="user-content-fnref-20" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">20</a></sup> Here the input is English, and a general model does the compiling.</p>
<p>Experiment 6 does the same thing with tools. Astra builds a 1,480,274-weight Go network through a Python sandbox, layering in liberties, captures, atari and eyes. With no forward passes it wins 42.6%, 26.3% and 36.3% of games against three PPO networks of the same size. With 50 forward passes it wins 74.2%, 70.0% and 78.5%.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-21" id="user-content-fnref-21" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">21</a></sup> Tree search isn&#x27;t allowed, and a fully connected MLP is a weak shape for Go, so the opponents aren&#x27;t strong. Experiment 4 is a one-shot edit to 6,073 of 171,664 weights that adds a back-up path to a Kinetix car policy. The authors report success going from 0% to 100%. adiant211 didn&#x27;t re-run that one, and neither did I.</p>
<p>Chandan Singh ran a related test with Claude on tasks with known algorithms.<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-22" id="user-content-fnref-22" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">22</a></sup> Across 959 written programs, all ten algorithmic tasks reached 100% within three attempts. His report also records a failure. On language tasks the agent drifted from writing mechanisms to fitting them, and on SNLI it set its readout with logistic regression on the evaluation set. Any harness that gives an agent many rounds of feedback can drift the same way.</p>
