utside critic</td><td><sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-30" id="user-content-fnref-30" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">30</a></sup></td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>The adiant211 result doesn&#x27;t make Astra&#x27;s run trivial. That pipeline starts from Astra&#x27;s final report, so it already knew the recipe, and its author says as much: a cheap model or a person picks the question and the recipe, and tools do the reading. Krentsel&#x27;s results, for their part, show that writing weights in one reply got much better in recent models. Neither shows a new way of understanding networks. Both things are true: the capability is new at this level, and the methods are old.</p>
<h2 id="where-natively-holds">Where &quot;natively&quot; holds</h2>
<p>The post&#x27;s title says Astra reads and writes weights natively. The experiments split into three groups:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>No tools, small networks.</strong> Experiments 1, 2, 4 and 5. This is close to native. The networks either have structure that shows in the weights or do a task that compiles to a short program.</li>
<li><strong>Python, no forward pass.</strong> Experiment 3. Astra does linear algebra in a sandbox and decides what to compute.</li>
<li><strong>Python plus forward passes.</strong> Experiments 6, 7 and 8. This is an agent doing interpretability with activations, generations and paired tests. The result lands in the weights, but the information comes from running the model.</li>
</ul>
<p>The Qwen result is in the third group. The post&#x27;s closing ideas, recursive self-improvement and grafting circuits from one model into another, build on results like that one. The Gemma result and the failed weights-only audit in Experiment 7 cut against that step, and I wouldn&#x27;t cite the post for those ideas yet.</p>
<p>These tests would settle more than another demo:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Remove the hint at full budget.</strong> Run prompt v4 at 50 passes with several seeds, and report every run, including the ones that submit nothing.</li>
<li><strong>Add a decoding baseline.</strong> Score the base and edited Qwen side by side with a repetition penalty or a loop detector as a third arm. That shows what the edit adds beyond a sampling change.</li>
<li><strong>Break the labels.</strong> Repeat Experiment 1 with the channel names removed and the channel order shuffled, so a norm map no longer tells you which channel is the agent.</li>
<li><strong>Blind the audit.</strong> Plant a backdoor with a known trigger. Give Astra the weights only, then probes, and report false positives on clean models. That tests the auditing claim directly.</li>
<li><strong>Lift the cap.</strong> Score Breakout without the 10,000-step limit and report survival and score rate separately.</li>
<li><strong>Use models with several failures.</strong> Gemma suggests a single-direction fix only works when a single direction carries the failure.</li>
</ol>
<p>The authors say they are building a benchmark and a harness. I hope it reports every run, fixes the prompts in advance and includes models with no easy bug. They released 1,125 audited turns with exact diffs and hashes,<sup><a href="gpt-6-astra-weight-space-programming#user-content-fn-2" id="user-content-fnref-2-2" data-footnote-ref="true" aria-describedby="footnote-label">2</a></sup> which is why I could check their work this closely. Most of what I&#x27;ve written here comes from their own files.</p>
<p>If you maintain small open models, you can use this now. A general agent with access to activations can find a rank-one fix for a known failure in about a day. Treat what it finds as a patch: keep the base checkpoint, hash the edit and run per-item regressions on the tasks you care about. And before you read a 6× gain as new skill, check how many answers were finishing before.</p>
