d 24%, MMLU-Pro 18%, AIME 2025 6%, AIME 2024 2%.\",caption:\"Computed from exp8_math/eval/unedited/merged.json and eval/p50c/merged.json, commit 4d09ba6. It assumes the gain comes from newly finished answers.\",width:1520,height:740,placeholder:\"data:image/webp;base64,UklGRjwAAABXRUJQVlA4IDAAAADQAQCdASoQAAgAA4BaJZwAAt0AkXxgAAD+9Vhhbq7YLhfHIXk0N6kQhS1Aul5RAAA=\",sources:[{type:\"image/avif\",srcset:\"https://media.bosnadev.com/hub/1ceb1a44e5cbf5cd/wsp-finished-answers-640x312.avif 640w, https://media.bosnadev.com/hub/423b5c0db76b8370/wsp-finished-answers-1280x623.avif 1280w, https://media.bosnadev.com/hub/1bc7811824dbc938/wsp-finished-answers-1520x740.avif 1520w\"},{type:\"image/webp\",srcset:\"https://media.bosnadev.com/hub/c60e33c609fbee96/wsp-finished-answers-640x312.webp 640w, https://media.bosnadev.com/hub/a58b84aaf4140a70/wsp-finished-answers-1280x623.webp 1280w, https://media.bosnadev.com/hub/701540310cc2bf2a/wsp-finished-answers-1520x740.webp 1520w\"}]}),`\n`,(0,t.jsxs)(e.p,{children:[\"This was the most useful number I got from the logs, because it shows how much ability the loop was hiding. On grade-school maths and MATH-500 it was hiding a lot. On AIME the edited model finishes a third of its answers and gets almost none right. On GPQA Diamond the newly finished answers score about what you'd get by guessing among four options. Part of the 3\\xD7 on IFEval is mechanical: the grader checks the text after \",(0,t.jsx)(e.code,{children:\"\x3C/think>\"}),\", so an answer that never closes its thinking has nothing to grade.\"]}),`\n`,(0,t.jsxs)(e.p,{children:[\"The Gemma 3 270M control fits this reading. Gemma rarely loops, only 8.5% of its answers reach the limit, and Astra's edits moved its MATH-500 score from 6.8% to at most 7.7%.\",(0,t.jsx)(e.sup,{children:(0,t.jsx)(e.a,{href:\"#user-content-fn-15\",id:\"user-content-fnref-15-2\",\"data-footnote-ref\":!0,\"aria-describedby\":\"footnote-label\",children:\"15\"})}),\" On a model without the loop, the same process found nothing to fix.\"]}),`\n`,(0,t.jsxs)(e.p,{children:[`Qwen's own model card warns about this. It says Qwen3.5-0.8B \"is more prone to entering thinking loops\", and that this happens even with the recommended sampling settings.`,(0,t.jsx)(e.sup,{children:(0,t.jsx)(e.a,{href:\"#user-content-fn-17\",id:\"user-content-fnref-17\",\"data-footnote-ref\":!0,\"aria-describedby\":\"footnote-label\",children:\"17\"})}),\" The evaluation used the card's top-k 20 and presence penalty 1.5, with temperature 0.6 instead of the card's 1.0. So the 7.9% baseline is a known weakness of this model, and Astra fixed it in the weights.\"]}),`\n`,(0,t.jsxs)(e.p,{children:[\"The fix is useful. It is permanent, it targets a real failure, and it was cheap. I added up the run's request files: the search used about 6,000 generations and 145M generated tokens. At the rate of the Hugging Face re-run, 43.4M tokens in 51 minutes on one H200,\",(0,t.jsx)(e.sup,{children:(0,t.jsx)(e.a,{href:\"#user-content-fn-3\",id:\"user-content-fnref-3-2\",\"data-footnote-ref\":!0,\"aria-describedby\":\"footnote-label\",children:\"3\"})}),\" that is about three GPU-hours plus Astra's own tokens. It needed no training, but it did get feedback from all those generations.\"]}),`\n`,(0,t.jsx)(e.h2,{id:\"writing-weights-from-scratch\",children:\"Writing weights from scratch\"}),`\n`,(0,t.jsxs)(e.p,{children:[\"Experiment 5 surprised me. Astra gets the game rules and the source file and writes all 20,486 weights of a 600 \\u2192 32 \\u2192 32 \\u2192 6 tanh network in one reply, with no tools, feedback or training. For Breakout the reply is 20,567 tokens long and 815 of the weights are nonzero.\",(0,t.jsx)(e.sup,{children:(0,t.jsx)(e.a,{href:\"#user-content-fn-18\",id:\"user-content-fnref-18\",\"data-footnote-ref\":!0,\"aria-describedby\":\"footnote-label\",children:\"18\"})}),\" adiant211 rebuilt the networks bit for bit and got the same scores.\",(0,t.jsx)(e.sup,{children:(0,t.jsx)(e.a,{href:\"#user-content-fn-7\",id:\"user-content-fnref-7-7\",\"data-footnote-ref\":!0,\"aria-describedby\":\"footnote-label\",children:\"7\"})})]}),`\n`,(0,t.jsx)(e.p,{children:\"The network reads like a compiled program. Four first-layer units compute where the ball will land for each direction it can travel, with the wall bounces written into the coefficients. The second layer decides between moving left and right.\"}),`\n`,(0,t.jsxs)(e.p,{children:[\"The post's headline here is 517.4 against 15.8 for PPO. The evaluation file shows where that ratio comes from.\",(0,t.jsx)(e.sup,{children:(0,t.jsx)(e.a,{href:\"#user-content-fn-19\",id:\"user-content-fnref-19\",\"data-footnote-ref\":!0,\"aria-describedby\":\"footnote-label\",children:\"19\"})}),\" All runs use sticky actions at 0.1, a 10,000-step cap and MinAtar 1.0.15:\"]}),`\n`,(0,t.jsxs)(e.table,{children:[(0,t.jsx)(e.thead,{children:(0,t.jsxs)(e.tr,{children:[(0,t.jsx)(e.th,{children:\"Breakout policy\"}),(0,t.jsx)(e.th,{children:\"Mean score\"}),(0,t.jsx)(e.th,{children:\"Mean episode length\"}),(0,t.jsx)(e.th,{children:\"Score per step\"})]})}),(0,t.jsxs)(e.tbody,{children:[(0,t.jsxs)(e.tr,{children:[(0,t.jsx)(e.td,{children:\"Astra, one reply\"}),(0,t.jsx)(e.td,{children:\"517.4\"}),(0,t.jsx)(e.td,{children:\"7,417\"}),(0,t.jsx)(e.td,{children:\"0.070\"})]}),(0,t.jsxs)(e.tr,{children:[(0,t.jsx)(e.td,{children:\"PPO, 500M steps, best seed\"}),(0,t.jsx)(e.td,{children:\"25.3\"}),(0,t.jsx)(e.td,{children:\"282\"}),(0,t.jsx)(e.td,{children:\"0.090\"})]}),(0,t.jsxs)(e.tr,{children:[(0,t.jsx)(e.td,{children:\"PPO, 100M steps, best seed\"}),(0,t.jsx)(e.td,{children:\"15.8\"}),(0,t.jsx)(e.td,{children:\"178\"}),(0,t.jsx)(e.td,{children:\"0.088\"})]})]})]}),`\n`,(0,t.jsxs)(e.p,{children:[\"PPO scores faster per step and then drops the ball. Astra's policy rarely loses the ball: 517 of its 1,000 episodes hit the 10,000-step cap.\",(0,t.jsx)(e.sup,{children:(0,t.jsx)(e.a,{href:\"#user-content-fn-7\",id:\"user-content-fnref-7-8\",\"data-footnote-ref\":!0,\"aria-describedby\":\"footnote-label\",children:\"7\"})}),\" With a longer cap the ratio would grow, so the 33\\xD7 tells you about survival and the cap more than about how good the policy is. It is still a network that reacts to one frame, with no loop and no simulator, written blind, and it keeps the ball in play for thousands of steps.\"]}),`\n`,(0,t.jsxs)(e.p,{children:[\"The other games keep this in proportion. PPO at 500M steps beats Astra on Seaquest (88.9 against 53.1) and narrowly on Asterix (55.2 against 53.2). These are small MLPs on 10 \\xD7 10 grids. A task that compresses into a short program can be written down as weights, and Tracr showed that compile step in 2023 with a formal language as input.\",(0,t.jsx)(e.sup,{children:(0,t.jsx)(e.a,{href:\"#user-content-fn-20\",id:\"user-content-fnref-20\",\"data-footnote-ref\":!0,\"aria-describedby\":\"footnote-label\",children:\"20\"})}),\" Here the input is English, and a general model does the compiling.\"]}),`\n`,(0,t.jsxs)(e.p,{children:[\"Experiment 6 does the same thing with tools. Astra builds a 1,480,274-weight Go network through a Python sandbox, layering in liberties, captures, atari and eyes. With no forward passes it wins 42.6%, 26.3% and 36.3% of games against three PPO networks of the same size. With 50 forward passes it wins 74.2%, 70.0% and 78.5%.\",(0,t.jsx)(e.sup,{children:(0,t.jsx)(e.a,{href:\"#user-content-fn-21\",id:\"user-content-fnref-21\",\"data-footnote-ref\":!0,\"aria-describedby\":\"footnote-label\",children:\"21\"})}),\" Tree search isn't allowed, and a fully connected MLP is a weak shape for Go, so the opponents aren't strong. Experiment 4 is a one-shot edit to 6,073 of 171,664 weights that adds a back-up path to a Kinetix car policy. The authors report success going from 0% to 100%. adiant211 didn't re-run that one, and neither did I.\"]}),`\n`,(0,t.jsxs)(e.p,{children:[\"Chandan Singh ran a related test with Claude on tasks with known algorithms.\",(0,t.jsx)(e.sup,